---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';

const page = {
  title: "Create a Windows 11 bootable USB on macOS — Bootable",
  description: "Create a Windows 11 USB installer on Apple Silicon macOS. Includes FAT32, split WIM files, and media checks.",
  ogTitle: "Create a Windows 11 bootable USB on macOS",
  ogDescription: "Create FAT32 Windows media and split oversized WIM files on macOS.",
  ogType: "article",
  current: "guide",
  doc: { label: "Safety", href: "https://github.com/debpalash/bootable/blob/main/docs/safety.md" },
  crumbs: [{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Guides", href: "/guides.html" }, { label: "Windows USB on macOS" }],
};
---
<Layout {...page}>
<main class="guide-main">
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={page.crumbs} />
      <article>
        <header class="guide-hero"><p class="eyebrow">Windows installer · macOS</p><h1>Create a Windows 11 USB on macOS</h1><p class="lede">Create FAT32 installer media from a Windows ISO. Split oversized WIM files when needed.</p></header>
        <div class="guide-body">
          <div class="guide-content">
            <h2 id="support">macOS support</h2>
            <p>Bootable supports Apple Silicon. It accepts whole removable drives and rejects the macOS system disk.</p>
            <p>Bootable mounts the ISO read-only. It creates GPT or MBR FAT32 media with <code>diskutil</code>.</p>

            <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
            <ul>
              <li>An Apple Silicon Mac.</li>
              <li>A trusted Windows 11 ISO.</li>
              <li>An expendable USB drive with enough capacity.</li>
              <li><code>wimlib</code> when the ISO contains an oversized WIM or CA 2023 files are requested.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="guide-commands"><pre><code>brew install wimlib
curl -fsSL https://bootable.palash.dev/install.sh | sh -s -- --gui</code></pre></div>
            <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>The release is not signed or notarized.</strong> Verify the release SHA-256 file before running it.</p></div>

            <h2 id="helper">Write helper</h2>
            <p>The installer adds the root-owned helper at <code>/Library/PrivilegedHelperTools/app.bootable.helper</code>. The desktop app runs without administrator access.</p>

            <h2 id="steps">Create the installer</h2>
            <ol>
              <li>Open the Windows 11 ISO in Bootable.</li>
              <li>Select the USB drive by its physical model and capacity.</li>
              <li>Choose GPT or MBR and the Windows setup options.</li>
              <li>Review the source, drive, operations, and erasure warning.</li>
              <li>Confirm erasure and authenticate.</li>
              <li>Keep the drive connected until the media check finishes.</li>
            </ol>

            <h2 id="verification">Media checks</h2>
            <p>Bootable checks the UEFI files, setup payload, split WIM parts, and FAT32 limits. A cancelled write is marked incomplete.</p>
            <p>This does not guarantee that Windows Setup will finish.</p>

            <section class="related-guides"><h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/verify-bootable-usb">Verification details</a></li><li><a href="/safe-usb-writer">Drive safety</a></li><li><a href="/windows-11-usb-on-linux">Windows USB on Linux</a></li></ul></section>
            <div class="guide-cta"><h2>Install on macOS</h2><p>Available for Apple Silicon.</p><a class="button primary" href="/download">View macOS install steps</a></div>
          </div>
          <aside class="guide-aside"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li><a href="#support">macOS support</a></li><li><a href="#requirements">Requirements</a></li><li><a href="#helper">Protected helper</a></li><li><a href="#steps">Create the installer</a></li><li><a href="#verification">Verification</a></li></ul></aside>
        </div>
      </article>
</main>
</Layout>
